'''''Psychrolutes marcidus''''', the '''smooth-head blobfish''',<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://bie.ala.org.au/species/urn:lsid:biodiversity.org.au:afd.taxon:d81ebbc4-fb09-4a72-afac-55a04b7305a1#names|title=''Psychrolutes marcidus'' – Names|publisher=Atlas of Living Australia|access-date=2019-03-28}}</ref> or simply the '''blobfish''',<ref name=":0" /> is a [[deep-sea fish]] of the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Psychrolutidae]]. It inhabits the deep waters off the coasts of mainland [[Australia]] and [[Tasmania]], as well as the waters of [[New Zealand]].<ref name=fishbase>{{FishBase|genus=Psychrolutes |species=marcidus|month=February|year=2010}}</ref>

Blobfish are typically shorter than {{convert|30|cm|in|abbr=on}}. They live at depths between {{convert|600|and|1200|m|ft|abbr=on}}, where the pressure is 60 to 120 times greater than that at [[sea level]], which would likely make [[gas bladder]]s inefficient for maintaining [[buoyancy]].<ref name=fishbase/> Instead, the flesh of the blobfish is primarily a gelatinous mass with a [[density]] slightly less than that of water; this allows the fish to float above the sea floor without expending energy on swimming. The blobfish has a relative lack of [[muscle]], but this is not a disadvantage, as its main food source is [[Marine snow|edible matter]] that floats in front of it, such as deep-ocean [[crustacean]]s.<ref>{{cite book |title=Unusual Creatures: A Mostly Accurate Account of Some of Earth's Strangest Animals |first=Michael |last=Hearst |year=2012 |publisher=Chronicle Books |pages=24–25 |url={{google books |plainurl=y |id=n9cNICADXMkC1blobfish%20diet |page=24}} |isbn=978-1-4521-0467-6}}</ref>

